  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Suppose that a matrix $B$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(5-\\lambda )^{3}(3+\\lambda )^{9}(\\lambda -\\pi )^{6},$ the nullity of $(B-\\pi I)^{3}$ equals $3,$ and the nullity of $(B-\\pi I)^{4}$ equals $5.$ If a basis of ${R}^{18}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $B$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\pi ?$",
  "worked_solution": "For the given matrix $B$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\pi ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(B-\\pi I)^{4}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(B-\\pi I)^{3}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{3}$ has dimension $3.$ This means that the solutions to $(B-\\pi I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $3.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{4}$ has dimension $5.$ This means that the solutions to $(B-\\pi I)^{4}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $5.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{3}$ is a subspace of dimension $3$ in $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{4}$, which is a subspace of dimension $5,$ our basis can contain only $2$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{4}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B-\\pi I)^{4}-\\text{nullity}(B-\\pi I)^{3}=5-3=2\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\pi .$",
